Variables 
  A variable is a place to store values. (Page 13) 

Displaying and Changing a Variable 

  All variables in Powershell start with a dollar sign($), which indicates to Powershell that you are calling a variable and not a cmdlet, function, script, or      executable file. 

  $MaximumHistoryCount

  The $MaximumHistoryCount variable is a built-in variable that determines the maximum number of commands PowerShell saves in its command history; the default
  is 4096 commands. 

  You can change a variable's value by entering the variable name-starting with a dollar sign- and then using the equal sign(=). 

  $MaximumHistoryCount = 200

  Variables in Powershell comin in two broad classes: user-defined varaibles, whcih are created by the user, and automatic variables, which already exist in Powershell. (Page 14)

User Defined Variables 

  A variable needs to exist before you can use it. To create a variable, you need to declare it-say it exists- and then assign it a value to it (or intialize it). 

  $color = 'blue'

  Once you've created teh variable and assigned it a value, you can reference it by typing the variable name in the console. 

  The value of a variable won't change unless something, or someone, explicitly changes it. 

  When you use the equal sign to define the variable, you're doing the same thing you'd do with the Set-Variable command. (Page 15)

  Set-Variable -Name color -Value blue

  Get-Variable -Name color

  Both output blue for the variable color. 

  -You can also use Get-Variable to return all available variables. 

Automatic Variables (Page 16)
  The $null varialbe is a strange one: it represents nothing. 

  $foo = $null
  $foo
  $bar

  Get-Variable -Name foo

  Another commonly used automatic variable is %LASTEXITCODE. Powershell allows you to invoke external exectuable applications like the old school ping.exe, 
  which pings a website to get a response. 
  When external applications finish running, they finish with an exit code, or return code, that indicates a message. 

  Typically, a 0 indicates success, and anything else means either a failure or anamoly. (Page 18) 

  The Preference Variable 

    Powershell has a type of automatic variable referred to as preferrence variables. These variables control the default behavior of various output streams: Error, Warning, Verbosem Debug, and information. (Page 18). 

    You can find a list of all the preference variables by running Get-Variable and filtering all variables ending in Preference. 

    Get-Variable -Name *Preference 

    These variables can be used to configure the various types of output Powershell can return. 

    


